* GIT — git for hash-file records, modelled on real git.  The "path" is an MVX
* file plus record id; the working tree is the live records.  GITIGNORE (an
* account record) keeps bulk data out of history; DICT addresses a dictionary.
*
* One source, both platforms: a CMD-framework dispatch to the GIT.* handlers.
* Each handler is IFDEF MVX (a one-line CALL into the engine, which reads records
* AND does the git objects) / ELSE (UniData: BASIC drives the record loop natively
* — OPEN/SELECT/READNEXT/READ/WRITE — and calls C only for git objects via CallC,
* no InterCall / second session).  The heavier record-I/O ops delegate their udt
* branch to GITUDT.ADD/STATUS/DIFF/CHECKOUT; the rest inline it.
* Platform compile-time defines (MV / UDT / UV / D3): a build-created, gitignored
* header in the BP.INC include file; on MVX it is comment-only (MVX is builtin).
$INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
COMMON /GITENV/ MVPK
MVPK = ""                            ;* init GIT.HAS's per-run lock-probe cache
* --- machine mode (-M): fence the output so a caller can find it ------------
* A program driving this verb — uv-git, which runs it inside a `uv` session —
* has to separate our output from everything else the session emits: the logon
* banner, the prompts, and whatever an account's LOGIN paragraph decides to
* print.  Scraping by content is guesswork, so with -M the verb marks its own
* boundaries and the caller keeps only what falls between them.
*
* The markers come from BASIC rather than TCL deliberately: there is no verb at
* the TCL prompt that reliably echoes a literal (DISPLAY is a paragraph
* statement, not a verb, in this flavour), but a program can always CRT one.
*
* The flag is stripped here and the cleaned sentence handed to GIT.SENT, so
* every handler parses what it would have without it.
COMMON /GITMACH/ GMACH, GSENT
GMACH = 0 ; GSENT = ""
CALL GIT.SENT(MSENT)
IF FIELD(MSENT, " ", 2) = "-M" THEN
   GMACH = 1
   GSENT = FIELD(MSENT, " ", 1) : " " : FIELD(MSENT, " ", 3, 99)
END
$IFDEF UDT
* --- machine mode turns the pager off --------------------------------------
* UniData paginates its own terminal output at the page depth TERM reports —
* "Enter <New line> to continue..." — and in a driven session there is nobody
* to answer it: the prompt takes the NEXT command off the input stack as its
* keystroke instead.  That is how `GIT -M ADD -A` followed by `GIT -M COMMIT`
* in one session ran the add and then silently never ran the commit.  It is
* not a keep-the-messages-short problem: any account whose own honest output
* fills a page re-creates it with no code change at all, so the verb turns
* paging off for itself rather than promising to stay brief.
*
* THREE things about this line are load-bearing; each was measured on 8.3.
*
* NO `CAPTURING`.  `EXECUTE ... CAPTURING` issued AFTER anything has been
* printed fires the pager itself, right there, and that prompt eats the next
* command — the exact bug, moved.  (Print first, then EXECUTE CAPTURING: one
* pager hit.  Either without CAPTURING, or before any output: none.)  This is
* what made an earlier attempt at this fix read as "EXECUTE consumes the input
* stack"; it is CAPTURING that does, and only after output.  TERM prints
* nothing when it is given parameters, so there is nothing to capture.
*
* BEFORE the fence, not after.  It has to run before we print, both for the
* rule above and so that anything the child does say lands outside the fenced
* region, where a machine-mode caller already discards it.
*
* `TERM ,0` and not `TERM 132,0`: only the depth is ours to change.  The width
* belongs to whoever set the session up, and a machine-mode caller wants the
* lines it would otherwise have got, not lines re-wrapped to a number we
* picked.
*
* UDT only.  UniVerse does not paginate a driven session at all (measured with
* both a dumb and a vt100 TERM), and its TERM verb REJECTS a depth of 0 —
* "must be an integer in the range 1 through 32767" — so the same line there
* would print an error and fix nothing.
IF GMACH THEN EXECUTE "TERM ,0"
$ENDIF
IF GMACH THEN PRINT "<<<GIT-BEGIN>>>"
* --- `--version` as a switch ------------------------------------------------
* `GIT VERSION` is a command and always has been; this is the spelling people
* type first, and git itself accepts.
*
* IT ANSWERS HERE AND STOPS, rather than rewriting the sentence for the
* framework: the framework parses the sentence ITSELF, so a verb that edits its
* own copy changes nothing the framework sees.  The first attempt at this
* (mv_git#85) did worse than nothing — it assigned the uppercased sentence to
* GSENT, which is the COMMON the handlers read, so `GIT COMMIT -m one` came back
* "unknown option 'M'".  The switch never even fired.  Answering and returning
* touches no shared state at all.
*
* The sentence comes from GIT.SENT — already read above — and NOT from
* @SENTENCE: how a sentence is reached differs per platform and between `GIT`
* and `GIT -M`, which is why that subroutine exists.
VSW = FIELD(TRIM(MSENT), " ", 2)
IF GMACH THEN VSW = FIELD(TRIM(GSENT), " ", 2)
IF OCONV(VSW, "MCU") = "--VERSION" THEN
*  Called by NAME, the way the framework calls every handler: a literal
*  `CALL GIT.VERSION` resolves to the symbol GITVERSION on MVX and does not
*  find it.  One dispatch mechanism, not two.
   VH = "GIT.VERSION"
   CALL @VH
   IF GMACH THEN PRINT "<<<GIT-END>>>"
   STOP
END
$IFNDEF MVX
* Off MVX the git objects live outside this session — CallC on UniData, the
* background process on UniVerse — and both fail silently when absent, so probe
* once and fail with that platform's own remedy rather than letting the first
* real operation quietly do nothing.
CALL GIT.REQUIREOBJ(OBJOK, OBJWHY)
IF NOT(OBJOK) THEN
   CALL GIT.ECHO(OBJWHY)
   STOP
END
$ENDIF
* --- cmd-framework dispatch (picks the installed cmd, else the bundled GIT.CMD.*)
* git bundles a cut-down cmd as GIT.CMD.*; when the mvpkg-installed cmd is present
* GIT.HAS("cmd") is true and we dispatch through the real CMD.* instead.  The set
* is chosen by name (CALL @), so there is no catalog-override to reason about.
CALL GIT.HAS("cmd", HASCMD)
IF HASCMD THEN PFX = "CMD." ELSE PFX = "GIT.CMD."
CI = PFX : "INIT" ; CA = PFX : "ADD" ; CR = PFX : "RUN" ; CF = PFX : "FLAG"
CALL @CI("GIT", "version-control hash-file records")
CALL @CA("INIT", "create the record repository", "GIT.INIT")
CALL @CA("ADD", "stage records (file {id})", "GIT.ADD")
CALL @CF("ADD", "A", "all", 0, "", "stage every changed record")
CALL @CF("ADD", "o", "open", 0, "", "open (portable) format")
CALL @CA("RM", "unstage a record (file id)", "GIT.RM")
CALL @CA("IGNORE", "keep records out of history (pattern)", "GIT.IGNORE")
CALL @CA("VERSION", "engine, libgit2 and its transports", "GIT.VERSION")
CALL @CA("STATUS", "show staged / changed records", "GIT.STATUS")
CALL @CA("COMMIT", "commit staged records (-m msg)", "GIT.COMMIT")
CALL @CF("COMMIT", "m", "message", 1, "", "commit message")
CALL @CA("LOG", "commit history", "GIT.LOG")
CALL @CA("DIFF", "record changes ({file}); --staged for the index", "GIT.DIFF")
CALL @CF("DIFF", "", "staged", 0, "", "diff the index against HEAD")
CALL @CF("DIFF", "", "cached", 0, "", "the same, spelled git's older way")
CALL @CF("DIFF", "u", "unified", 0, "", "unified output, with hunk headers")
CALL @CA("SHOW", "committed content of a record (file id)", "GIT.SHOW")
CALL @CA("RESTORE", "restore records from HEAD (file)", "GIT.RESTORE")
CALL @CA("BRANCH", "list or create a branch (name)", "GIT.BRANCH")
CALL @CA("CHECKOUT", "switch branch, update records (name)", "GIT.CHECKOUT")
CALL @CA("MERGE", "merge a branch into this one (name)", "GIT.MERGE")
CALL @CA("CHERRY-PICK", "apply one commit (commit)", "GIT.CHERRYPICK")
CALL @CA("CLONE", "clone a repo into a dir (url dir {ref})", "GIT.CLONE")
CALL @CA("FETCH", "fetch a remote ({remote})", "GIT.FETCH")
CALL @CA("PULL", "fetch + merge, re-materialising ({remote} {branch})", "GIT.PULL")
CALL @CA("IMPORT", "re-materialise a file's records from HEAD (file)", "GIT.IMPORT")
CALL @CA("PUSH", "push to a remote ({remote} {refspec})", "GIT.PUSH")
CALL @CA("REMOTE", "list or configure remotes ({add|set-url|remove} name {url})", "GIT.REMOTE")
CALL @CA("CONFIG", "get or set repo config (key {value})", "GIT.CONFIG")
CALL @CA("ATTR", "view or edit account / file attributes ({file})", "GIT.ATTR")
CALL @CF("ATTR", "s", "set", 1, "", "set a parameter (name=value)")
CALL @CF("ATTR", "u", "unset", 1, "", "remove a parameter (name)")
CALL @CF("ATTR", "", "sync", 0, "", "adopt the live file's geometry as an edit")
CALL @CF("ATTR", "e", "edit", 0, "", "full-screen editor")
CALL @CA("TAG", "list or manage tags (name {commit} | -a name -m msg | -d name)", "GIT.TAG")
CALL @CF("TAG", "a", "annotate", 1, "", "create an annotated tag (name)")
CALL @CF("TAG", "m", "message", 1, "", "annotation message")
CALL @CF("TAG", "d", "delete", 1, "", "delete a tag (name)")
CALL @CR
* The closing fence.  Its ABSENCE is meaningful to the caller: it means the verb
* did not finish — a handler that STOPped, or a session that never reached us —
* so an empty result cannot be mistaken for a clean run.
IF GMACH THEN PRINT "<<<GIT-END>>>"